  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Find the set of \\(x\\)-values satisfying the inequality \\[ \\left|\\frac{5-x}{3}\\right|\\lt2. \\] [The symbol \\(|a|\\) means \\(+a\\) if \\(a\\) is positive, \\(-a\\) if \\(a\\) is negative, \\(0\\) if \\(a\\) is zero. The notation \\(1\\lt a\\lt2\\) means that \\(a\\) can have any value between \\(1\\) and \\(2,\\) excluding \\(1\\) and \\(2.\\)]",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(1\\lt x\\lt11\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(-1\\lt x\\lt11\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(x\\lt11\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(x>11\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(|x|\\lt6\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1960-p26.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The inequality is equivalent to \\[ |5-x|\\lt6, \\] so \\(-6\\lt5-x\\lt6.\\) Subtracting \\(5\\) and reversing signs as needed gives \\[ -1\\lt x\\lt11. \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Multiply the inequality by \\(3\\)",
        "Rewrite \\(|5-x|\\lt6\\) as a compound inequality"
      ],
